{
  "dom_id": "step-q5831",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability of getting $4$ on the first toss and then $5$ on the second toss?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $A$ the event \"we get the number $4$ on the first toss\" and $B$ the event \"we get the number $5$ on the second toss.\" Then the required probability is $P(A\\cap B).$\n\nSince die throws are independent events, we can use the multiplication law for independent events, which states that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=P(A)\\cdot P(B).\n$$\n\nLet's find $P(A)$ and $P(B).$ There are $6$ possible outcomes when we roll the die. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above into the multiplication law for independent events, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
